1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,989
Moving an Orchard Core site to production means changing a few things that

2
00:00:05,989 --> 00:00:07,673
work on your machine.

3
00:00:07,673 --> 00:00:09,668
This video is a checklist:

4
00:00:09,668 --> 00:00:14,034
configuration, a reverse proxy, HTTPS, and data protection keys.

5
00:00:14,034 --> 00:00:16,840
Each item shows what to set, and where.

6
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:25,841
On your machine, the site runs in the Development environment and reads its

7
00:00:25,841 --> 00:00:28,068
settings from files in the project.

8
00:00:28,068 --> 00:00:33,990
In production, the environment is Production, and values such as connection strings come from the host.

9
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:39,756
Requests may reach the site through a reverse proxy, and visitors expect HTTPS.

10
00:00:39,756 --> 00:00:44,064
And when more than one server runs the site, the servers must share

11
00:00:44,064 --> 00:00:45,893
the data protection keys.

12
00:00:45,893 --> 00:00:48,490
The next chapters take these in order.

13
00:00:52,690 --> 00:00:58,136
In Program.cs, the Orchard Core web project registers the exception handler for the

14
00:00:58,136 --> 00:01:01,868
Error path whenever the environment is not Development.

15
00:01:01,868 --> 00:01:06,457
On the server, set the ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT variable to Production.

16
00:01:06,457 --> 00:01:10,740
The same name selects files such as appsettings.Production.json.

17
00:01:11,490 --> 00:01:16,216
Orchard Core reads its settings from the OrchardCore section of appsettings.json.

18
00:01:16,216 --> 00:01:18,878
A production database is a typical example:

19
00:01:18,878 --> 00:01:23,822
DatabaseProvider and ConnectionString at the root of that section are the default for

20
00:01:23,822 --> 00:01:29,200
every tenant, and environment variables provide them with a double underscore between levels.

21
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:34,633
Keep credentials in the environment of the host, not in committed files.

22
00:01:34,633 --> 00:01:40,696
The shipped appsettings.json also carries a commented AllowedHosts line, which its comment says

23
00:01:40,696 --> 00:01:42,977
to set on public-facing servers.

24
00:01:42,977 --> 00:01:47,420
Separate videos cover the configuration sources and Azure Key Vault.

25
00:01:48,170 --> 00:01:54,641
Set the ORCHARD_APP_DATA variable to move the App_Data folder, using a relative or an absolute path.

26
00:01:54,641 --> 00:01:59,402
The folder holds the tenants file and, for each tenant, its settings and

27
00:01:59,402 --> 00:02:05,140
data protection keys, plus Lucene indexes and a SQLite database when the site uses them.

28
00:02:09,379 --> 00:02:15,354
Behind a reverse proxy, every request reaches the site from the proxy, not from the visitor.

29
00:02:15,354 --> 00:02:21,512
The Reverse Proxy Configuration feature, under Tools, Features, lets you choose which forwarded

30
00:02:21,512 --> 00:02:23,829
HTTP headers the site processes.

31
00:02:24,306 --> 00:02:26,953
Its page is under Settings, Reverse Proxy.

32
00:02:26,953 --> 00:02:29,104
There is one box for each header:

33
00:02:29,104 --> 00:02:34,673
X-Forwarded-For for the client address, X-Forwarded-Proto for the protocol the client used, and

34
00:02:34,673 --> 00:02:37,816
X-Forwarded-Host for the host the client asked for.

35
00:02:38,273 --> 00:02:42,249
Under each box, the page shows the value of the current request:

36
00:02:42,249 --> 00:02:45,260
the client address, the protocol, and the host.

37
00:02:45,260 --> 00:02:49,294
After you deploy, open the page through your proxy to check them.

38
00:02:49,294 --> 00:02:51,453
Let's turn on all three headers.

39
00:02:51,914 --> 00:02:56,313
Saving can restart the website, as the warning on the page says, and

40
00:02:56,313 --> 00:02:58,453
a message confirms the change.

41
00:02:58,453 --> 00:03:02,614
These settings belong to the tenant, so each tenant has its own.

42
00:03:04,214 --> 00:03:11,187
The Reverse Proxy Configuration feature is ordered before CORS, the Security module, and authentication.

43
00:03:11,187 --> 00:03:13,405
The source gives the reasons:

44
00:03:13,405 --> 00:03:19,934
CORS needs the correct host, and the proxy must be configured before authentication and security.

45
00:03:20,684 --> 00:03:24,233
The page has no field for known proxies or networks.

46
00:03:24,233 --> 00:03:31,086
Call ConfigureReverseProxySettings in the host, and set KnownProxies and KnownNetworks in the ReverseProxy

47
00:03:31,086 --> 00:03:33,900
section, with networks in CIDR notation.

48
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:36,164
Those values override the page.

49
00:03:36,914 --> 00:03:40,890
In production, always list the proxies and networks you trust.

50
00:03:40,890 --> 00:03:46,210
The documentation warns that improperly configured settings can expose the site to IP

51
00:03:46,210 --> 00:03:48,314
spoofing and header injection.

52
00:03:52,542 --> 00:04:00,381
With the HTTPS feature enabled under Tools, Features, its page appears under Settings, Security, HTTPS.

53
00:04:00,381 --> 00:04:06,709
Opened over plain HTTP, the page warns that changing HTTPS-only settings over HTTP

54
00:04:06,709 --> 00:04:12,822
has been prevented, and it disables the two check boxes and the SSL Port field.

55
00:04:13,297 --> 00:04:17,345
Require HTTPS redirects all requests to HTTPS.

56
00:04:17,345 --> 00:04:23,260
To change any of these settings, open the page over HTTPS, because the

57
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:26,997
page does not save changes made over HTTP.

58
00:04:27,456 --> 00:04:29,693
HSTS mode has three options:

59
00:04:29,693 --> 00:04:32,720
Disabled, Enabled, and From environment:

60
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:35,879
enabled in Production, disabled otherwise.

61
00:04:35,879 --> 00:04:41,538
The red notice warns that browsers may stop connecting over HTTP after receiving

62
00:04:41,538 --> 00:04:45,026
the header, if HTTPS later becomes unavailable.

63
00:04:45,490 --> 00:04:53,010
Require HTTPS Permanent redirects with the permanent status code 308, instead of a temporary redirect.

64
00:04:53,010 --> 00:04:55,934
SSL Port is the port to redirect to.

65
00:04:55,934 --> 00:05:00,320
Leave it empty, and the middleware tries to determine it.

66
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:05,949
A recipe sets the same values with the Settings step.

67
00:05:05,949 --> 00:05:14,553
HttpsSettings takes StrictTransportSecurityMode, where FromConfiguration is the option the page labels From environment,

68
00:05:14,553 --> 00:05:18,650
plus RequireHttps, RequireHttpsPermanent, and SslPort.

69
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:26,203
When HSTS is on, the header has a maximum age of 365 days and covers subdomains.

70
00:05:26,203 --> 00:05:29,050
The preload option is not set.

71
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:40,417
ASP.NET Core Data Protection protects authentication cookies, anti-forgery tokens, and persisted secrets that

72
00:05:40,417 --> 00:05:43,534
must be decrypted, such as an SMTP password.

73
00:05:43,534 --> 00:05:48,706
By default, each tenant keeps its keys in a DataProtection-Keys folder in its

74
00:05:48,706 --> 00:05:50,700
own folder under App_Data.

75
00:05:51,450 --> 00:05:56,766
If the keys are lost, existing cookies and tokens stop working, so back up the folder.

76
00:05:56,766 --> 00:06:01,098
And when several servers run the site, each server would have its own

77
00:06:01,098 --> 00:06:03,640
keys, so they must share one key ring.

78
00:06:04,390 --> 00:06:09,100
The Distributed Data Protection (Azure Storage) feature keeps the key ring in an

79
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:11,017
Azure Blob Storage container.

80
00:06:11,017 --> 00:06:14,632
Set ConnectionString in the Azure section of DataProtection.

81
00:06:14,632 --> 00:06:19,396
The container is named dataprotection by default, and the feature creates it when

82
00:06:19,396 --> 00:06:20,710
it does not exist.

83
00:06:21,460 --> 00:06:26,714
By default, each tenant's key ring is a blob named DataProtectionKeys.xml, in a

84
00:06:26,714 --> 00:06:29,556
folder named for the tenant under Sites.

85
00:06:29,556 --> 00:06:35,489
ContainerName and BlobName accept Liquid templates that use ShellSettings, so a tenant can

86
00:06:35,489 --> 00:06:37,590
have a container of its own.

87
00:06:38,340 --> 00:06:44,303
The Distributed Data Protection (Redis) feature keeps the key ring in Redis, configured

88
00:06:44,303 --> 00:06:46,034
in the Redis section.

89
00:06:46,034 --> 00:06:51,290
Redis must persist data, with AOF or RDB, because a key ring is not a cache.

90
00:06:51,290 --> 00:06:54,880
The site logs a warning if it cannot confirm that.

91
00:06:55,630 --> 00:06:58,435
After enabling either feature, check the log.

92
00:06:58,435 --> 00:07:03,220
Without a connection string, the Azure feature logs a critical error, and without

93
00:07:03,220 --> 00:07:06,685
a valid Redis configuration, the Redis features stay off.

94
00:07:06,685 --> 00:07:09,600
The site then keeps using the keys in App_Data.

95
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:16,595
Before you go live, confirm four things.

96
00:07:16,595 --> 00:07:22,124
The environment is Production, with settings from the host, and the forwarded headers

97
00:07:22,124 --> 00:07:24,615
and known proxies match your proxy.

98
00:07:24,615 --> 00:07:29,110
HTTPS is required, and every server shares the data protection keys.

99
00:07:29,860 --> 00:07:31,739
Most items can be scripted.

100
00:07:31,739 --> 00:07:37,430
The host supplies the environment variables, the configuration files carry the data protection

101
00:07:37,430 --> 00:07:41,984
settings, and the Settings step of a recipe sets the forwarded headers and

102
00:07:41,984 --> 00:07:43,520
HTTPS on each tenant.

103
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:47,166
That's the checklist:

104
00:07:47,166 --> 00:07:54,116
a Production environment, settings from the host, forwarded headers, HTTPS, and shared data protection keys.

105
00:07:54,116 --> 00:07:55,700
Thanks for watching.

